        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "> > This is a feature and a fix. It may be entirely unnecessary if nobody has complained about it, and I know we're hoping to deprecate vim.opt anyways, but following up on [#39849 (comment)](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/39849#discussion_r3271868956).\r\n> \r\n> is it potentially useful for `vim.o` ? given the proposal in [#20107 (comment)](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/20107#issuecomment-1240069511)\r\n\r\nProbably not. I think this is mostly useful for maintaining intermediate values, but I don't see how that's useful for vim.o since we don't want infix ops there (we want to use normal Lua table manipulation) (right?). I don't expect normal users will really have to pass this parameter since they can just rely on the default (existing option value). Unless you mean `vim.o().listchars` would return a magic metatable; I assumed you meant it would return a normal table like `{ eol = '~', space = '-' }`.",
